WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
This cell tore open.

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.800
What killed it: an accident, or a program?

3
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.860
Chapter one showed two endings: an apoptotic cell held its membrane until eaten; a

4
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.200
necrotic cell tore early.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.140
For decades, these two looks were taught as a pair.

6
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:26.100
Apoptosis, named in 1972 by Kerr, Wyllie and Currie, was the programmed death.

7
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.980
Necrosis was the accident.

8
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.760
That pairing has not survived.

9
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:36.113
A regulated death runs on dedicated machinery.

10
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:40.353
Genes or drugs can turn that machinery up or down.

11
00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:43.493
Such a cell can still look necrotic.

12
00:00:44.773 --> 00:00:47.353
Three such deaths are defined by what they depend on.

13
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:49.853
First, necroptosis.

14
00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:55.093
Activated RIPK3 adds phosphate to MLKL.

15
00:00:56.393 --> 00:01:00.053
Clusters of MLKL then move to the plasma membrane.

16
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:03.153
Second, pyroptosis.

17
00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:08.933
Gasdermins are cut, freeing fragments that form pores in the plasma membrane.

18
00:01:10.253 --> 00:01:13.833
It often, but not always, follows inflammatory caspases.

19
00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:20.627
Third, ferroptosis, driven by severe, iron-dependent damage to membrane lipids.

20
00:01:22.187 --> 00:01:26.487
GPX4 is the main guard, reducing damaging lipid hydroperoxides.

21
00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:30.827
In cultured cells, drugs that trap iron block it.

22
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.487
Here is the idea to carry.

23
00:01:34.807 --> 00:01:37.027
A death's look names an endpoint, not a cause.

24
00:01:38.307 --> 00:01:41.907
To find the cause, block the machinery and see whether the death changes.

25
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.320
Now take dying cells in a dish, with no phagocyte.

26
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.240
Two dyes read chapter one's two membrane events.

27
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.220
Annexin V binds phosphatidylserine it can reach from outside.

28
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.900
Propidium iodide, a DNA dye, is kept out by an intact membrane.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A flow cytometer reads both colours on each cell and places it in one of four boxes.

30
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.440
An apoptotic cell in a dish often becomes flagged while intact.

31
00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:17.380
After secondary necrosis, it is flagged and leaky.

32
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.720
The boxes are staining states, not verdicts.

33
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.180
Other markers each report one event.

34
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.060
TUNEL labels free DNA ends, whatever broke them.

35
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.040
Cleaved caspase-3 shows the protease switched on, not that it killed.

36
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.980
LDH, a cytosol enzyme, leaks through a torn membrane.

37
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:42.153
So does LDH mean a cell died?

38
00:02:43.993 --> 00:02:50.193
Researchers removed NINJ1, a small plasma membrane protein, from mouse macrophages.

39
00:02:51.513 --> 00:02:52.633
Then they triggered death.

40
00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.073
Will LDH pour out?

41
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:02.053
Very little came out.

42
00:03:03.153 --> 00:03:07.013
The cells died, but they stayed swollen, like balloons that never popped.

43
00:03:08.453 --> 00:03:10.673
Death and the final tear are separate steps.

44
00:03:12.053 --> 00:03:14.313
So LDH measures the tear, not the death.

45
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:16.853
So back to the torn cell.

46
00:03:18.173 --> 00:03:20.953
Its look tells you how it ended, not what killed it.

47
00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:26.073
To find the killer, block each candidate mechanism and watch what changes.
